### The cleansing of Haifa

Moredechai Maklef, then the operations officer of the Carmeli Brigade and later the Israeli army Chief of Staff, issues orders to his troops:

> Kill any Arab you encounter; torch all inflammable objects and force doors open with explosives.

Thousands of Palestinians flee their homes, with little time to bring their belongings. They flee homes with food on the table and toys on the floor. By the morning of the 22nd, Palestinians gather in the harbor seeking escape.

The Carmeli brigade stations 3-inch mortars on the slopes overlooking the harbor and bombard the Palestinians below.

A survivor recalls:

> Men stepped on their friends and women on their own children. The boats in the port were soon filled with living cargo. The overcrowding in them was horrible. Many turned over and sank with all their passengers.

The British Field-Marshal Montgomery defends British army's decision to withdraw, leaving Palestinians defenceless. The last communication from the Palestinian leaders in Haifa to the British commander stationed there reads:

> We feel distressed and profoundly aggrieved by the lack of sympathy on the part of the British Authorities to render aid to the wounded although they have been requested to do so.